# Example only, not a hardened distribution. If your setup drives the create-only snapshot request through
# sudo instead of (or alongside) group membership on the requests directory, this is the shape of the rule:
# exactly one command, no arguments the agent controls beyond what request-snapshot.sh itself takes, no
# wildcard.
#
# Install as /etc/sudoers.d/oncall-snapshot-door (mode 440, validated with `visudo -c -f`):

{{ONCALL_USER}} ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /opt/agent-guardrails/bin/request-snapshot.sh /var/lib/snapshot-gate/requests /var/lib/snapshot-gate/states *

# The trailing `*` only matches the optional timeout argument request-snapshot.sh itself accepts (see
# snapshot-gate/README.md) -- it is not a green light for arbitrary arguments to an arbitrary command. Prefer
# the group-membership approach in snapshot-gate/install-snapshot-gate.sh when you can: this sudoers rule
# exists for setups where that is not an option. Replace {{ONCALL_USER}} with the real account name, and test
# with denial-probe.sh's "add itself to a sudoers-like capability" probe afterwards.
